        ],
        "params": {
          "text": "**CT06 \u2014 Can increasing feedback gain move stable poles outside the unit circle?**  \n**Predict \u2192 Run \u2192 Inspect \u2192 Change gain.** Run 32 s at 10 Hz. Open both Scopes. The two forward-Euler lag states supply causal memory; command has no actuator limiter.  \nClosed poles z=0.9 \u00b1 0.1i\u221aK. K=4 is stable; K=16 rings longer; K=19 is marginal, K=25 grows. Stability is |z|<1, not Re(z)<0.  \nSelect gain, F4: value 4 \u2192 16; save/reopen/rerun. All signals use normalized unit 1.  \nSource/state sample n is observed at Scope time (n+1)/10 s. Each lag updates from the same old states; do not add a feedback delay.  \n[Open CT06 student lab](qrc:/s7studio/help/education/labs/CT06-closed-loop-poles/README.md) \u00b7 [MIT root locus](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-06-principles-of-automatic-control-fall-2012/resources/mit16_06f12_lecture_11/) \u00b7 [MIT stability margins](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-06-principles-of-automatic-control-fall-2012/resources/mit16_06f12_lecture_23/)",
          "text_format": "markdown",
          "width": 1700,
          "height": 440,
          "font_size": 20
        }
      }
    ]
